00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.260
Erin Kennedy wants to know this:

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:21.190
Could you please explain the significance

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:24.230
of the heliosphere, and how it's made?

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:25.063
- [Neil] So the sun, as we know,

00:05:25.063 --> 00:05:26.520
is a ball of gas,

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.470
but it's not just all stays that way.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:31.440
Some gas gets, like, ejected.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.340
And so there's a stream of particles

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.173
that come from the sun

00:05:34.173 --> 00:05:35.880
that we call the solar wind.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:37.800
And you might have
heard of the solar wind,

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.570
'cause it makes aurora.

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.550
The particles stream and collide

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.170
with our atmosphere near the poles,

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:44.170
and it renders it a glow.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:45.130
Very beautiful.

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:46.730
Well, this solar wind moves

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:49.920
completely through the solar system,

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.000
and it goes beyond the planets,

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.140
beyond the comets,

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:56.400
and there is a point,
where this solar wind

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.050
can no longer be distinguished from

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:01.880
the median that permeates between

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:03.717
the stars of the galaxy.

00:06:03.717 --> 00:06:08.717
That is the actual edge
of the sun's influence.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:10.890
The sun's sort of particle influence

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:12.710
on its environment.

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:16.223
And we call the size of
that volume the heliosphere,

00:06:16.223 --> 00:06:20.530
and the boundary of that
volume the heliopause.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:24.280
And on September 12th, 2013, Voyager One,

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:25.940
the intrepid Voyager One,

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:28.370
crossed the heliopause,

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.200
and only then could you say,

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.180
it has left the solar system entirely.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.520
It's not just, oh, let's
go beyond the last planet,

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:38.830
and now you've left the solar system, no.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:41.610
No, the sun reaches out far beyond.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:44.510
And there you have the heliosphere.

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:45.790
And it's always there, by the way.

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:47.190
And as we move through the galaxy,

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:49.110
it can take on different shapes,

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:51.890
depending on what's going on around us,

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:53.872
and depending on the strength of the wind

00:06:53.872 --> 00:06:55.650
at any given moment.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:56.550
So it could be a sphere,

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:58.580
but it's usually a teardrop,

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:00.369
as the solar system moves,

00:07:00.369 --> 00:07:03.090
as we orbit the center of the galaxy,

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:04.440
the Milky Way galaxy.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:05.273
Do you have it, Chuck?

00:07:05.273 --> 00:07:06.780
- All right, Neil, that's the heliosphere,

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:07.910
but can you answer this?

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.380
What is the craposphere?

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:11.370
No you can't, 'cause I just made it up.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:12.590
It's my life.

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:14.320
- I got nothing for you on that.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:16.080
- All right, now, on a serious note,

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:18.480
you know we did an
episode about asteroids,

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:20.720
and I actually saw this comment

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.300
in the Facebook group comments.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:25.810
Ray Parker said this:

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.410
I don't care or want to
hear about asteroids.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:30.560
If we get hit, so be it,

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.020
nothing we can do about it.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:32.990
There are other things

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:37.250
that are a lot more important than rocks.

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:38.083
Neil.

00:07:38.083 --> 00:07:39.280
- [Neil] This is why you want

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:42.010
scientists and engineers in your midst.

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:43.890
Because when something that might

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:46.890
render you extinct arises,

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:48.430
is on its way towards Earth,

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:50.570
they will look up and say,

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:53.230
how can we prevent that?

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:55.320
And they'll use methods and tools,

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.690
and ingenious ideas,

00:07:57.690 --> 00:07:59.771
and that can be the difference

00:07:59.771 --> 00:08:03.850
between you being alive,
saying you don't care,

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:05.580
and you being extinct,

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:08.610
where your thoughts then
wouldn't matter to anyone.

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:10.920
- [Chuck] Hey man, nobody
can argue with that.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.770
I'm picking the reality where

